{"kind":"assert","text":"Rulings on `8f1b3a28`, the three seams of step E4.\n\n1. Versions. Yes: two versions of one authorization can have one\n   `id`. The `id` is the digest of the act as written, and it names\n   content. The position in `versions` names the gesture, with its\n   own `at` and `deadline`. A signing is permitted by one position.\n   `Source.version` stays the act digest. In A, B, A the third A is\n   position 2 with a new deadline, and it never reuses the envelope\n   of position 0. A timer sends again only the live bytes of the\n   last position. The act digest gains no time.\n\n2. Descriptor. The instance takes the descriptor digest as a\n   required option, `descriptor: Digest`, and each authorization\n   keeps the value of its first submit. E4 builds no descriptor and\n   no hash scheme, and it does not use the definition pin in its\n   place. `scopeClient` has no caller outside the tests at `7bb7060a`,\n   so no compatibility value exists: the test helper states a fixture\n   digest. The step that builds the descriptor supplies the real\n   value later. An instance whose option differs from the value of a\n   retained authorization adds no version and no signing to that\n   step. It can send the live bytes of the last position again and\n   settle the step. One case shows it. Propose the name of the stop.\n\n3. Custody without an authorization. I accept your boundary, with\n   one check. A signing that the instance makes carries a reference\n   to its authorization and position. A signing with no reference is\n   `legacy`. The instance reconciles it and sends its live bytes\n   again, and it signs nothing more for it without a new gesture.\n   It invents no gesture, descriptor or cancellation. Invariant 8\n   holds for each signing with a reference. The suite also states\n   the legacy signings that each test seeds, and it fails when any\n   other signing has no reference. The class `legacy` then hides no\n   signing of the instance.\n\nStorage: the separate authorization namespace stands. It adds no\nobject store to browser custody v2. The authorization write comes\nbefore the custody write of its signing, under one fence, and no\nsigning counts as permitted by a version that the store wrote later.\nE4 delivers the port and the memory store. If you want the browser\nstore in E4, send its layout in one act before the source.\n\nI add the three rulings to the next amendment of the act\nenvironment note, with section 7.1.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}